"Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta-analysis (https://chernev.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/ChoiceOverload_JCP_2015.pdf)\nciteturn20view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 26; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://chernev.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/ChoiceOverload_JCP_2015.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2124\nL0@P0: Research Review\nL1@P0: Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta-analysis\nL2@P0: Alexander Chernev a,⁎, Ulf Böckenholt a, Joseph Goodman b\nL3@P0: a Kellogg School of Management, Northwestern University, USA b Compass Lexecon, Chicago, USA\nL4@P0: Received 5 October 2012; received in revised form 18 August 2014; accepted 23 August 2014\nL5@P0: Available online 29 August 2014\nL6@P0: Abstract\nL7@P0: Despite the voluminous evidence in support of the paradoxical finding that providing individuals with more options can be detrimental to\nL8@P0: choice, the question of whether and when large assortments impede choice remains open. Even though extant research has identified a variety of\nL9@P0: antecedents and consequences of choice overload, the findings of the individual studies fail to come together into a cohesive understanding of when\nL10@P0: large assortments can benefit choice and when they can be detrimental to choice. In a meta-analysis of 99 observations (N = 7202) reported by\nL11@P0: prior research, we identify four key factors—choice set complexity, decision task difficulty, preference uncertainty, and decision goal—that\nL12@P0: moderate the impact of assortment size on choice overload. We further show that each of these four factors has a reliable and significant impact on\nL13@P0: choice overload, whereby higher levels of decision task difficulty, greater choice set complexity, higher preference uncertainty, and a more\nL14@P0: prominent, effort-minimizing goal facilitate choice overload. We also find that four of the measures of choice overload used in prior research—\nL15@P0: satisfaction/confidence, regret, choice deferral, and switching likelihood—are equally powerful measures of choice overload and can be used\nL16@P0: interchangeably. Finally, we document that when moderating variables are taken into account the overall effect of assortment size on choice\nL17@P0: overload is significant—a finding counter to the data reported by prior meta-analytic research.\nL18@P0: © 2014 Society for Consumer Psychology. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nL19@P0: Keywords: Choice overload; Assortment; Decision complexity; Meta-analysis\nL20@P0: Contents\nL21@P0: Introduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 334\nL22@P0: The pros and cons of large assortments . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 334\nL23@P0: Choice overload in consumer decision making . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 335\nL24@P0: Choice overload in individual decision making . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 335\nL25@P0: Conceptualizing the impact of assortment size on choice overload . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 336\nL26@P0: Decision task difficulty . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 337\nL27@P0: Choice set complexity . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 337\nL28@P0: Preference uncertainty . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 338\nL29@P0: Decision goal . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 339\nL30@P0: Method . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 339\nL31@P0: The data . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 339\nL32@P0: The model . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 340\nL33@P0: Results . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 344\nL34@P0: Model fit . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 344\nL35@P0: Effects of the specific moderators of choice overload . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 344\nL36@P0: ⁎ Corresponding author. Fax: +1 847 491 2498.\nL37@P0: E-mail address: ach@kellogg.northwestern.edu (A. Chernev).\nL38@P0: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jcps.2014.08.002\nL39@P0: 1057-7408/© 2014 Society for Consumer Psychology. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nL40@P0: Available online at www.sciencedirect.com\nL41@P0: ScienceDirect\nL42@P0-1: Journal of Consumer Psychology 25, 2 (2015) 333–358The mean effect of assortment size on choice overload . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 346\nL43@P1: The effect of assortment size across individual experiments . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 348\nL44@P1: Publication bias . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 350\nL45@P1: Discussion . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 351\nL46@P1: Key findings . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 351\nL47@P1: Prior meta-analytic research . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 352\nL48@P1: Future research . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 353\nL49@P1: Appendix A. Overview of the analyzed studies . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 354\nL50@P1: Appendix B. Reanalyzing the data from prior meta-analytic research . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 356\nL51@P1: References . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 354\nL52@P1: Introduction\nL53@P1: The importance of assortment decisions for both retailers and\nL54@P1: manufacturers has been underscored by numerous research\nL55@P1: articles, marketing textbooks, and the popular press (Iyengar,\nL56@P1: 2010; Levy & Weitz, 2006; Schwartz, 2003). Because of its\nL57@P1: importance, the topic of how product assortment influences\nL58@P1: consumer choice has generated a substantial amount of interest\nL59@P1: across different research domains, including economics, analytical\nL60@P1: and empirical modeling, individual and group decision making,\nL61@P1: and social psychology (Broniarczyk, 2008; Chernev, 2012; Kahn,\nL62@P1: 1999; Kahn, Weingarten, & Townsend, 2013; Lancaster, 1990;\nL63@P1: Lehmann, 1998; Simonson, 1999).\nL64@P1: Within assortment research, the topic of the negative\nL65@P1: consequences of large assortments has attracted a disproportion\u0002ate amount of interest among researchers. This interest can be\nL66@P1: attributed largely to the paradoxical finding that variety can be\nL67@P1: detrimental to choice, which challenged the conventional wisdom\nL68@P1: that providing consumers with more options always facilitates\nL69@P1: choice (Iyengar & Lepper, 2000; Reibstein, Youngblood, &\nL70@P1: Fromkin, 1975). Building on these findings, recent research has\nL71@P1: moved beyond simply documenting choice overload to identify\u0002ing its antecedents and boundary conditions. In doing so,\nL72@P1: researchers have identified a number of important moderators\nL73@P1: of choice overload, such as attribute alignability (Gourville &\nL74@P1: Soman, 2005), consumer expectations (Diehl & Poynor, 2010),\nL75@P1: availability of an ideal point (Chernev, 2003b), personality traits\nL76@P1: and cultural norms (Iyengar, Wells, & Schwartz, 2006), option\nL77@P1: attractiveness (Chernev & Hamilton, 2009), decision focus\nL78@P1: (Chernev, 2006), construal level (Goodman & Malkoc, 2012),\nL79@P1: time pressure (Haynes, 2009), product type (Sela, Berger, & Liu,\nL80@P1: 2009), consumer expertise (Mogilner, Rudnick, & Iyengar,\nL81@P1: 2008), and variety seeking (Oppewal & Koelemeijer, 2005).\nL82@P1: Despite the voluminous evidence that large assortments can\nL83@P1: lead to choice overload, the question of whether and when large\nL84@P1: assortments are detrimental to choice remains open. Indeed, even\nL85@P1: though extant research has identified a variety of antecedents and\nL86@P1: consequences of choice overload, the individual studies use\nL87@P1: diverse independent and dependent variables. As a result, the\nL88@P1: findings of these studies fail to come together in a cohesive\nL89@P1: understanding of whether and when assortment size is likely to\nL90@P1: lead to choice overload. The goal of our research, therefore, is to\nL91@P1: identify factors that reliably moderate the impact of assortment\nL92@P1: size on choice overload and generalize them into an overarching\nL93@P1: conceptual framework. To achieve this goal, we abstract from the\nL94@P1: specific variables and manipulations reported in the individual\nL95@P1: studies to identify the key drivers of choice overload, quantify the\nL96@P1: effect sizes associated with these factors, and evaluate their\nL97@P1: impact on choice overload.\nL98@P1: Our analysis is organized as follows. First, we discuss the pros\nL99@P1: and cons of large assortments, focusing on how assortment size\nL100@P1: influences individual decision processes. This is followed by a\nL101@P1: conceptual analysis of the antecedents of choice overload, in\nL102@P1: which we identify four key drivers that are likely to influence the\nL103@P1: impact of assortment size on choice overload. We then present our\nL104@P1: methodology in more detail, followed by a summary of our key\nL105@P1: findings. This research concludes with a discussion in which we\nL106@P1: highlight our theoretical contributions, discuss the managerial\nL107@P1: implications, and outline directions for future research.\nL108@P1: The pros and cons of large assortments\nL109@P1: Offering consumers a large variety of options to choose from\nL110@P1: can have a two-pronged impact on choice: It can both benefit and\nL111@P1: hinder choice. The most intuitive benefit, featured prominently in\nL112@P1: economics research, is that the greater the number of options in the\nL113@P1: choice set, the higher the likelihood that consumers can find a close\nL114@P1: match to their purchase goals (Baumol & Ide, 1956; Hotelling,\nL115@P1: 1929). A related economic explanation of consumer preference for\nL116@P1: larger assortments involves the greater efficiency of time and effort\nL117@P1: involved in identifying the available alternatives in the case of\nL118@P1: one-stop shopping associated with retailers offering larger\nL119@P1: assortments (Betancourt & Gautschi, 1990; Messinger &\nL120@P1: Narasimhan, 1997).\nL121@P1: It has also been proposed that larger assortments might lead to\nL122@P1: stronger preferences because they offer option value (Reibstein et\nL123@P1: al., 1975), allow consumers to maintain flexibility in light of\nL124@P1: uncertainty about future tastes (Kahn & Lehmann, 1991;\nL125@P1: Kahneman & Snell, 1992; Kreps, 1979), and accommodate\nL126@P1: consumers' future variety-seeking behavior (Inman, 2001; Kahn,\nL127@P1: 1995; Levav & Zhu, 2009; Ratner, Kahn, & Kahneman, 1999;\nL128@P1: Van Herpen & Pieters, 2002). It has further been argued that\nL129@P1: consumers might experience additional utility simply from having\nL130@P1: multiple items in the choice set because it creates the perception of\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn20view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view0\",\"id\":15}); Total lines: 1\n"
